/**
 * The successful result of a computation that has no meaningful payload.
 *
 * `Unit` is both the type name and its single value, just as in Kotlin. An
 * omitted function return type means `Unit`; `return Unit` and a bare `return`
 * are therefore the same successful result. Backends may erase the singleton
 * from a function ABI, but it remains a first-class value to Azora code.
 */
@Since("0.1")
bridge pack Unit

/**
 * The bottom type: no value has it, and nothing can be read at it.
 *
 * It is a subtype of every type, just as in Kotlin, because control cannot
 * produce a `Nothing` value and then continue. Non-returning functions and
 * unrecoverable termination use it, and an expression branch of `Nothing`
 * leaves the other branch to determine the expression's type. `Nothing?` has
 * exactly one possible value: `null`.
 *
 * A compile-time computation also answers `Nothing` when the question it was
 * asked has no answer - the type of a default that was never declared, say.
 * `Unit` would be wrong there: it says there is one successful value, while
 * `Nothing` says there is no value at all.
 */
@Since("0.1")
bridge pack Nothing

/**
 * The erased top type accepted by APIs that can receive values of any type.
 *
 * `Any` is compiler-provided: it has no constructible runtime pack and each
 * backend uses its native erased value representation.
 */
@Since("0.1")
bridge pack Any

@Since("0.1")
bridge pack Self

/**
 * The compile-time meta-type: the type of a type. Used as the element type of
 * type lists (`[Type]`) and as the parameter type of `type` functions
 * (`type promote(T: ...Type)`).
 */
@Since("0.1")
bridge pack Type

/**
 * The type's own name, as a string: `T.typeName` is `"Position"` where `T`
 * was bound to `Position`.
 *
 * The receiver is written as a type because there is no value to read it
 * from - the member belongs to the type itself. Folded where `T` stops
 * being a parameter, which is the only place the answer exists: nothing
 * afterwards remembers what a caller chose.
 */
impl Type {
    /**
     * The type's own name, as a string: `T.typeName` is `"Position"` where
     * `T` was bound to `Position`.
     *
     * Folded where `T` stops being a parameter - an inline call site, or a
     * specialization - which is the only place the answer exists: a type
     * parameter has no name of its own, only the name of whatever a caller
     * chose for it, and nothing afterwards remembers which that was.
     */
    @Since("0.1")
    bridge deepinline prop &.typeName: String
}

@Since("0.1")
variant enum Option<T> {
    Some(T)
    None
}

deepinline func<T> Nullable(b: Bool): Type {
    return when b {
        true => Option<T?>
        false => Option<T>
    }
}

